# In situ rapid versatile method for the preparation of zirconium metal-organic framework filters

1School of Materials Science and Engineering, China University of Petroleum, Qingdao 266580, China; $^2.$ Beijing Key Laboratory of Photoelectronic/Electrophotonic Conversion Materials, Key Laboratory of Cluster Science, Ministry of Education, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Beijing Institute of Technology, Beijing 100081, China;   
3Key Laboratory of Magnetic Molecules & Magnetic Information Materials (Ministry of Education), Institute of Chemistry and Culture, School of Chemistry & Material Science, Shanxi Normal University, Taiyuan 030006, China
The development of the rapid preparation of highly stable metal-organic framework (MOF)-based devices provides the possibility of meeting the increasing demands of MOF in industrial applications. However, MOFs experience poor processability and stable high-valence-metal(IV)-based MOFs favor forming either thermodynamically stable metal hydroxides or oxides during their growth and nucleation, which hinders their practical applications. Herein, we present a versatile deep eutectic solvent (DES)- assisted hot pressing method to in situ rapidly prepare six distinct zirconium-based MOF nanocrystals on fibers (denoted as ZrMOFilters) within $20\mathrm{min}$ . A small amount of DES promotes MOF precursor contact and accelerates $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF growth. Temperature and pressure facilitate the formation of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs onto desired substrates. In situ $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance spectra and time-dependent Fourier-transform infrared spectra were conducted to elucidate the growth of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF nanocrystals. As a proof-of-concept, the abilities of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ and micro(nano) plastics removal have been demonstrated. This strategy paves the way for the rapid fabrication of highly stable MOF-based devices and brings MOFs a step closer to practical application.
in situ, UiO-66, DES, hot pressing, filter, pollutant control
