## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous crystalline materials constructed by metal ions and organic ligands [1,2]. They have been immensely studied due to their rich structural topologies and designable functionalities, offering great potential for water treatment [3], gas separation and purification [4,5], energy storage [6], and catalysis [7,8]. MOFs built up from high-valence-metal(IV) with inherent stability in structure and composition show the most potencial in practical applications [9]. Among the investigated MOFs, zirconium-based MOFs (Zr-MOFs) have attracted tremendous attention because of their outstanding physical and chemical stability and easy modification [10–14]. However, $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF synthesis often exists competitive reactions, with kinetic and thermodynamic products possible, demonstrating that fastidious conditions are required for their synthesis [15,16]. Solvothermal synthesis is the very common way to prepare Zr-MOFs, but its application for large-scale $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF production is not feasible as it requires a long reaction time of several days and uses large amounts of harmful solvents [15]. Furthermore, the inherent brittle nature of MOFs can easily break down into tiny particles and clog the reactors during flushing with fluid, which restricts their industrial applications [17,18]. Therefore, a scalable, rapid, facile, and environment-friendly approach to fabricate crystalline $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs into devices is highly desired for industrial processes.
Various approaches have been developed to fabricate ZrMOF devices, for example, by electrochemical deposition [11], secondary growth on seeding layers [19], step-by-step liquid-phase epitaxial growth [20], and colloidal dip-coating deposition [21] for in situ growing or depositing MOFs on porous substrates, or blending MOFs with additives [5,22] and controlling the specific MOF structures to get MOF membranes [23–26]. However, there are still unsurmountable challenges: (1) these methods suffer from tedious time/energy-consuming processes and require specific reaction equipment; (2) MOF layers suffer from poor stability and fragility; (3) binders or additives can block the pores or channels of MOFs, leading to the reduced performance. Hence, a versatile method is crucial to accommodate the maximum number of MOFs with simple equipment and directly turn the precursors into MOF devices. Recently, a solvent- and binder-free method, in situ hot-pressing (HoP) method, has been reported to directly turn MOF precursors into MOF coatings on various substrates, which is efficient for zeolite imidazole frameworks (ZIFs) preparation on commercial substrates, but non-applicability to high-valence-metal-based MOFs (such as $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs) [27,28]. Thus, an approach to directly turn $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF precursors into $Z\mathrm{r}.$ - MOF devices could be satisfactorily applied in industrial applications, but achieving rapid and facile $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF device preparation remains a considerable challenge.
Ionic liquids (ILs) are composed entirely of paired ions with high ionic conductivity and negligible vapor pressure, and have gained tremendous attention over the past few decades [29,30]. ILs have been utilized as solvents and structure-directing agents in porous materials synthesis [30– 33]. 1-Hexyl-3-methylimidazolium chloride, as a kind of ILs, was reported to significantly facilitate the formation of UiO-66 at room temperature in $30\mathrm{min}$ [33]. Nevertheless, most ILs are high prices, hazardous toxicity, and poor biodegradability, and their synthesis is far to be environmentfriendly, which hampers their industrial applications. To overcome these drawbacks, deep eutectic solvent (DES) composed of hydrogen bond acceptor (HBA) and hydrogen bond donor (HBD) can be considered as candidates for the synthesis of porous materials due to their similar physicochemical properties to ILs [34,35]. DES is obtained by simply mixing two safe, cheap, renewable, and biodegradable components [36], and has been reported as a medium and efficient structure-directing agent to synthesize trivalentmetal-based $(\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Y}}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Nd}}^{3+}$ , $\mathbf{\bar{S}m}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Gd}^{3\mathbf{\bar{+}}}$ , $\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Ho}}^{3+}$ , and ${\Upsilon}{\ b}^{3+})$ ) MOFs [37,38]. Recently, although DES was used as a benign medium for preparing UiO-66 at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $96\mathrm{h}$ [39], the utilization of a large amount of DES and long reaction time still restrict its real application.
Herein, we propose the DES-assisted HoP method for in situ rapidly turning paste powder of the starting materials into stable high-valence-metal(IV)-based MOF devices ( $\mathrm{Zr^{\_}}$ MOFilters). In this process, choline chloride (ChCl), a cheap, biodegradable, and non-toxic quaternary ammonium salt, and urea are chosen to act as HBA and HBD, respectively, for the rapid forming of a classic DES. Six $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters with high crystallinity are fabricated by choosing different Zr-MOF precursors under $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 1). A small amount of DES was exploited to enhance the diffusion of metal ions and accelerate the growth of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF nanocrystals and can be completely removed after synthesis. HoP method facilitates the formation of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs from the MOF precursors onto desired substrates by reacting with the functional groups on the surface of substrates (such as polyethylene terephthalate nonwoven fabric and anodic aluminum oxide). The developed porosity, high durability, ease of processing, and rich surface chem-physical properties of Zr-MOFs endow $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters to serve as filters for efficient removal of micro(nano) plastic $(97.5\%\pm2\%$ ) and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ ( $98.5\%\pm1.3\%\$ in water or seawater conditions.
## 2 Results and discussion
UiO-66, a classic ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r-MOF}$ with the formula $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}.$ - $\mathrm{(BDC)}_{6}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaBDC}=1{,}4$ -benzene-dicarboxylate) [15], was synthesized on polyethylene terephthalate nonwoven fabric taking the advantage of the DES-assisted HoP method by the reaction of zirconium oxychloride $(\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and sodium terephthalate $\left(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)$ with the aid of acetic acid (HAc) as a modulator. DES can significantly accelerate the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs formation, and temperature and pressure can improve the attachment on substrates thus enhancing the resilience of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs on nonwoven cloth. Optimization of the reaction conditions (including the dosage of DES and precursors, temperature, and time) to obtain high-quality crystals on non-woven cloth is a prerequisite for studying the performance. $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ is easily available from $\mathrm{{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ coordinated with HAc, and subsequently, its capping terminal ligands can be easily exchanged with the bridging ligands [11]. Our initial attempts used 1 mmol ${\mathrm{ZrOCl}}_{2}{\cdot}8{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 1 mmol $\mathrm{\DeltaNa_{2}B D C}$ mixed with $3~\mathrm{mL}$ HAc and $1\mathrm{mL}$ DES, and HoP at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $0.4\mathrm{MPa}$ to afford UiO-66 filters. Surprisingly, the powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of UiO-66 filters confirmed the formation of UiO-66 but with poor crystallinity compared with solvothermal UiO66 (Figure S1, Supporting Information online). Subsequently, the dosage of acetic acid, the amount of DES, reaction temperature, and time were investigated to determine the optimized conditions for the formation of UiO-66 filter (Figure 2a, Figures S2–S5). The optimal condition was 1 mmol $Z_{\mathrm{{rOCl}_{2}\cdot8H_{2}O}}$ , 1 mmol $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ mixed with $1~\mathrm{mL}$ HAc and $1\mathrm{mL}$ DES, and HoP at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ under $0.4\mathrm{MPa}$ followed by washing with ethanol to remove unreacted precursors to form UiO-66 filter. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) peak patterns of as-synthesized UiO-66 filter within $20\mathrm{min}$ by the DES-assisted HoP method agreed well with simulated and solvothermal UiO-66 (Figure 2a). The full-width halfmaximum (FWHM) of the main peak of the as-synthesized UiO-66 filter at $7.3^{\circ}$ declines within $20\mathrm{min}$ and keeps remained thereafter (Figure 2b and Figure S5), indicating that the crystallinity of the UiO-66 filter increases with the extended reaction time. The Fourier-transform infrared attenuated total reflectance (FTIR-ATR) spectra of UiO-66 filters, UiO-66, and non-woven cloth show the chemical bonds retained in UiO-66 filters for each component (Figure S6). $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms prove the highly porous characteristic of UiO-66 on nonwoven cloth and it should be noted nonwoven cloth is nonporous, revealing that the intrinsic porosities of UiO-66 are fully accessible (Figure S7). The loading of UiO-66 on the UiO-66 filter was calculated based on the weight loss of pure UiO-66 (Figure S8 and Table S1). Field-emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM) images and elemental mappings exhibit that UiO-66 particles gradually appeared on the smooth surface of the non-woven fabric, and UiO-66 nanoparticles (ca. $250\mathrm{nm}$ ) are uniformly distributed on nonwoven cloth within $20\mathrm{min}$ at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S9). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were conducted to determine the chemical states and surface composition of atoms in UiO-66 filters (Figure S10a–d). The binding energy of $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ in the UiO-66 filter is shifted compared with $\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Figure S10b), while the binding energy of O 1s in the UiO-66 filter is higher than that of $\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ (Figure S10c), indicating the formation of the framework, and the absence of Na 1s and Cl 2p peaks prove that there are no sodium ions and DES left in the UiO-66 filters (Figure S10a, d). Furthermore, the highresolution XPS spectra for O 1s of UiO-66 filter show three peaks centered at 530.5, 531.9 and $533.3\mathrm{eV},$ which can be ascribed to the oxygen atoms in the bridging linked $(\boldsymbol{\upeta}^{3}$ -O$\mathbf{Zr}_{3,\cdot}$ ), coordinated carboxylate group and free carboxylate group, respectively (Figure S11) [40,41]. In situ $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance $\mathrm{^{\circ}H_{\ l}M R)}$ spectra of UiO-66 synthesis by DES-assisted HoP method were conducted in dimethyl sulfoxide- $.d_{6}$ $(\mathrm{DMSO-}d_{6})$ . A new wide peak centered at $6.5\mathrm{ppm}$ chemical shift appeared compared with DES (Figure 2c, d), which can be ascribed to urea combined with active hydrogen atom leading to the breakage of the hydrogen bond of DES [42]. Moreover, the choline chloride (HBA) can induce $\mathrm{BDC}^{2-}$ to coordinate with $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ and significantly promote the formation of UiO-66 nanocrystals [33,43]. Particularly, we investigated the heating time effects on the UiO-66 formation under the temperature of $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ by in situ time-dependent FTIR measurements on a self-made heating stage (Figure S12). The peaks at 708, 746 and $824~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ appear at $15\mathrm{min}$ and become apparent with prolonged time, which can be attributed to the $-\mathrm{OH}$ and $-\mathrm{CH}$ bending mixed with $Z\mathrm{r-O}$ in $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{6}$ (Figure 2e) [44]. It should be noted that the bands suffer a blue shift compared with pure UiO-66 due to the existence of hydrogen bond in DES [45], and the UiO-66 filters synthesized by the DES-assisted HoP method after washing with ethanol exhibited similar FTIR-ATR spectra to solvothermal UiO-66, indicating the formation of UiO-66 within $20\mathrm{min}$ (Figure S6). The successful preparation of the UiO-66 filter demonstrates the effectiveness of the described DES-assisted HoP approach to regulate the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs assembly, suggesting its potential practicability for direct preparation of other ZrMOFilters using different organic ligands.
To demonstrate the advantages of the DES-assisted HoP strategy, we further grew other Zr-MOFs, including UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}(\mathrm{Br})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}(\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{2}.$ , and MOF-801 on nonwoven cloth (Figure 3). These employed $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters can be easily scaled up and retrofitted in industrial processes (Figure 3a–c and Figure S13). The PXRD patterns of the as-prepared $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters are consistent with those of pure $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF single crystals and simulation (Figure 3d), and FTIR-ATR spectra are retained for each component (Figure S14), indicating that the DES-assisted HoP method is applicable to different kinds of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters. Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) were conducted in $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere demonstrating that all $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFiltes exhibit good thermal stability (Figure S15). The $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF loadings on ZrMOFilters were calculated based on the weight change before and after growing Zr-MOFs (Table S1, Supporting Information online). Notably, ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption tests verify that the porosity of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs is retained in all cases since the nonwoven cloth is nonporous (Figure S16 and Table S2). More importantly, this general procedure can also grow Zr-MOFs on anodic aluminum oxide (AAO), which offers a great potential to grow MOF on natural fibers (e.g., cotton) or synthetic fibers (e.g., nylon, polyester) (Figure S17). These results indicate that the DES-assisted HoP method is a facile way to prepare $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters, which can supply great potential for water pollutant control.
Toxic metal ions and micro(nano) plastics are widespread in seawater and receive increased attention [46,47]. Recently, micro(nano) plastics have been detected in marine lives, and together with adsorbed toxic metal ions exhibit a potential threat to human organs and tissues [48,49]. Filtration system has advantages of simple and low-cost that could be easily carried out to remove toxic metal ions $(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-})$ . The as-synthesized $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters show efficient removal of toxic metal ions $(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-})$ and various types of simulated micro(nano) plastics in distilled water and seawater on a syringe filter system with a fixed flux of $165\mathrm{Lm}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ (Figure S18). Two layers of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters were soaked in water under ultrasound for $10\mathrm{min}$ to remove the surface attachments before filtrating. The MOF-801 filter shows the highest $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ rejection $(98.5\%\pm1.3\%)$ among the as-synthesized $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters, filter paper, microfiltration membrane and the non-woven cloth (Figure 4a and Table S3). The adsorption kinetic data of
Poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF, $1,000,000\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ was selected as the simulated micro(nano) plastic with ultrasound for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to form a homogeneous aqueous suspension 1 $\stackrel{\cdot}{\sim}350\mathrm{nm}$ size through SEM and dynamic light scattering tests) (Figure S24). All $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters show better performance than non-woven cloth, which stems from the unique properties endowed by $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs (Figure 4a). Particularly, the MOF-801 filter outperforms other filters (such as filter paper and polyethersulfone (PES) microfiltration membrane), exhibits high plastic removal efficiency (up to $97.5\%$ $\pm2\%$ ) and can be reused over 10 times (Figure 4a, Figures S25 and S26, Table S6). Raman mapping was performed on a quadrilateral-shaped region on the nonwoven fabric after filtration of PVDF (1,000,000), as highlighted in a red box in Figure $\scriptstyle4\mathrm{c}$ . The Raman intensity maps of $1{,}475\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with the least overlap show the inhomogeneous distribution of Raman intensities (Figure 4c and Figure S27), indicating that the micro(nano)plastics were arrested and attached to the surface of the filter, which is also proven by SEM images (Figure S28). Despite the openings of the nonwoven cloth larger than micro(nano) plastics and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ , the MOFilters with diverse characteristics are proved to be effective filters for pollutant capture (Figure S28).
The pollutants captured by $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters mainly relied on four mechanisms (Figure 4d): (1) electrostatic interactions with $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs; (2) interacting with the functional groups on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs or substrates; (3) hydrogen bond with MOFs; (4) pores of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs. We used $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs as a platform to elucidate the driving forces between $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs and pollutants (including micro(nano) plastics and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ ) and utilized $Z\mathrm{r}.$ MOFilters for integrated water cleaning. As for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ , the surface of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs offers a positive charge improving the electrostatic interactions (Figure 4e). Moreover, the H-bond interaction between the $\mathrm{~H~}$ atom of MOF-801 and the O atom of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ can also enhance the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ capture [50]. The FTIR-ATR spectra of MOF-801 after $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ adsorption exhibit that the peak for $\upmu{-}\mathrm{OH}$ at $471~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ was weakened, demonstrating $\upmu\mathrm{-OH}$ plays an important role in the $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ capture (Figure S29) [50]. Moreover, UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and UiO$66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ filters exhibit high $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ removal efficiency due to the hydrogen bond of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ with OH and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups. The pore structure of the MOF-801 filter can be easily recovered after cleaning with water and HCl solution after one cycle (Figure S30).
The removal performances of different types of micro (nano) plastics with diverse particle sizes were investigated. MOF-801 filter exhibits higher removal efficiency of PVDF $\left(400,000\ \mathrm{g\mol}^{-1}\right)$ , ${\sim}290\mathrm{nm},$ , polystyrene (PS, ${\sim}270\mathrm{nm})$ , polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE, ${\sim}230\mathrm{nm}$ ) and polyethylene (PE, ${\sim}310\ \mathrm{nm},$ than that of nonwoven cloth (Figures S23 and S31). The $\zeta$ potentials of UiO-66, $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , UiO-66- $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , UiO- $66\mathrm{-Br}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}(\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{2}$ and MOF-801 dispersed in water at $\mathsf{p H}7$ were measured (Figure 4e), which exhibit $17.1\pm0.3$ , $11.6\pm0.2$ , $18.8\pm0.6$ , $9.4\pm1.0$ , $13.1\pm0.3$ and $22.3\pm1~\mathrm{mV},$ respectively. Furthermore, the $\zeta$ potentials of PE, PTFE, PS, PVDF (400,000) and PVDF (1,000,000) are $-24.4\pm1.8,-28.9\pm1.0,-16.2\pm0.3,-34.9\pm2.0$ and $-25.6$ $\pm0.5\mathrm{mV}$ (Figure 4e), respectively. It is clear that the surface charge plays a significant role in enhancing the adhesion of micro(nano) plastics onto $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters. In addition, owing to the existence of OH and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups, micro(nano) plastics like PVDF and PTFE with $\mathrm{CF}_{2}$ groups can form hydrogen bonds with $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs leading to their high performance, but the removal efficiencies are still lower than that of the UiO66 filter due to their low zeta potential (Figure 4e and Table S6). We further set out to explore the filtration performance of seawater (containing $1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ PVDF 1,000,000 and $20\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}{\mathrm{Cr}_{2}}{\mathrm{O}}_{7}^{2-})$ by MOF-801 filter. The micro(nano) plastic removal efficiency can be maintained at $98\%\pm1.5\%$ and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ rejection can be up to $90\%\pm\:1.2\%$ , and the contaminant accumulated on the surface can be easily removed (Figures S32 and S33). These results indicate that the DES-assisted HoP strategy is a convenient way to directly fabricate $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs into devices with porosity and distinctive properties for seawater pollutant control and offers great opportunities for the comprehensive treatment of seawater.
## 3 Conclusions
In conclusion, a facile and versatile approach, the DES-assisted HoP method, is proposed to directly utilize precursors to fabricate $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF nanocrystals on diverse substrates. By virtue of their developed porosity, high durability, and easy processability, high rejection rates for micro(nano) plastics and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}$ in water and seawater are achieved. The versatility of the DES-assisted HoP method makes it easy to fabricate a maximum number of MOFs with simple equipment and directly turn the MOF precursors into MOF-based devices. We believe that other applications beyond wastewater treatment could be tackled by developing tailor-made MOF devices using the presented approach. The in situ rapid fabrication procedure of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFilters offers the prospect for promoting the industrialization process of MOFs and opens interesting possibilities in porous material device fabrication directly from precursors.
